1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:13,720
 And to live...

2
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:45,460
 ...Catholic Majesty Isabella, King Ferdinand of Spain,

3
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:48,120
 inhabitants of the town of Palos are ordered...

4
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:48,670
 Oh, let's go!

5
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:52,450
 ...to provide and fit out two ships within ten days.

6
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:56,940
 They will be prepared and put at the disposal of Senor Columbus, who is here present.

7
00:00:57,720 --> 00:01:00,710
 We would pre-size within a delay of ten days.

8
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:06,690
 Look at my boat. She needs repairs. They'll take three months.

9
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:09,190
 Why not come back when she's seaworthy?

10
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:12,000
 Requisition my boat? Oh, no, Senor, you may not.

11
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:15,040
 No, my boat happened to be in England. I'm sorry, Senor, you're out of luck.

12
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:21,940
 The Majesties, we requisition your ships now.

13
00:01:22,380 --> 00:01:26,140
 You want to requisition my ships, Signor? Go on, take them. I'm only too delighted.

14
00:01:44,190 --> 00:02:05,200
 No doubt about it. Enormous riches to explore. Wealth beyond dream.

15
00:02:05,540 --> 00:02:07,740
 And how many souls to convert to our holy church.

16
00:02:08,030 --> 00:02:09,460
 What do you think, Martin Pinzon?

17
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:15,880
 Signor Columbus, do you really believe it possible the lands of the East, Congo, could be so close to the lands of the West?

18
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:18,830
 I shall speak of it with my brother Vincente. I believe he will agree.

19
00:02:18,830 --> 00:02:21,030
 Which an adventure is indeed tempting.

20
00:02:21,780 --> 00:02:24,240
 See, the Nina and the Pinta are small ships, but sturdy.

21
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:27,620
 They will hold to the course. So be it.

22
00:02:27,960 --> 00:02:30,310
 I will command the Pinta and my brother, the Nina.

23
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:31,070
 We all agree?

24
00:02:31,390 --> 00:02:32,050
 Yeah, agree.

25
00:02:32,290 --> 00:02:32,700
 Sounds good.

26
00:02:33,860 --> 00:02:39,340
 And we have managed to convince our Basque to give us a ship for the voyage.

27
00:02:39,640 --> 00:02:51,750
 It's called the Marigallot, for our crews.

28
00:02:51,870 --> 00:02:52,730
 Where will we get them?

29
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:55,920
 There are plenty of idle sailors around, the Desacupados.

30
00:02:56,520 --> 00:02:59,820
 Yes, but the men know how many deaths there have been on these voyages.

31
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:03,960
 No, even gallowsbirds run the other way when we try to recruit them.

32
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:06,540
 It's that that makes the enterprise difficult.

33
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:07,820
 You'll need to panic, lads.

34
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:08,940
 I'll bring them around for you.

35
00:03:09,220 --> 00:03:12,780
 See, pangos, where you'll be headed, everybody knows that the roofs are made of gold.

36
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,220
 You'll all get richer than you have ever dreamed.

37
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:22,180
 Each man will have a daily ration of a pound of biscuits, plenty of meat, and fish.

38
00:03:22,770 --> 00:03:27,890
 Oil and vinegar, cheese, vegetables, lots of them, onions, real onions, and much more, you hear me?

39
00:03:28,280 --> 00:03:31,970
 And good wine, two bottles, daily. Lord forgive me.

40
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:40,260
 Yes, you heard right, two bottles of wine every day, and you will get a bonus in advance.

41
00:03:40,890 --> 00:03:43,030
 That's an offer I can't refuse. I'm going.

42
00:03:46,620 --> 00:05:38,200
 Thank you so much.

43
00:05:49,870 --> 00:05:51,510
 They don't look as if they'd cause any harm.

44
00:05:51,670 --> 00:05:52,290
 They're Jews.

45
00:05:52,660 --> 00:05:54,330
 Versos, it's the will of the Holy Inquisition.

46
00:06:13,950 --> 00:06:14,990
 Excellency, King's notary.

47
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:18,680
 Excellency, the King's steward.

48
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:20,860
 Your Excellency, Supervisor of Equipment.

49
00:06:21,400 --> 00:06:23,020
 Welcome, the God of all provisions.

50
00:06:23,690 --> 00:06:24,910
 Welcome, King's interpreter.

51
00:06:25,540 --> 00:06:25,980
 Interpreter?

52
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:29,720
 Sure, he's supposed to speak Greek, Hebrew, Arabic, and all the rest.

53
00:06:29,980 --> 00:06:31,120
 He's polyglot.

54
00:06:38,980 --> 00:06:43,460
 His name hoist up the sails.

55
00:06:43,460 --> 00:06:45,980
 All hands and God's name hoist the sails.

56
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:47,920
 In the name of God, hoist sails.

57
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:54,010
 You surely saw the ship drift.

58
00:07:55,050 --> 00:07:58,590
 Seeing his majesty's orders, I shall have to place you under arrest.

59
00:07:58,760 --> 00:07:59,450
 You will follow us.

60
00:07:59,710 --> 00:08:02,950
 Do you really think we'll allow you to get away with this?

61
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:03,840
 Another question.

62
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:05,250
 Maybe now you ought to arrest.

63
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:09,040
 Was there an emergency where we let it go?

64
00:08:18,100 --> 00:08:39,580
 You've been eating.

65
00:08:40,530 --> 00:08:43,220
 You managed to seal some grub while we were all sleeping.

66
00:08:43,590 --> 00:08:44,770
 Only you must be starved.

67
00:08:45,430 --> 00:08:48,090
 Now listen, we'll see that you're all right and we'll give you eat.

68
00:08:48,340 --> 00:08:50,730
 I'll tell a few of the lads that we can trust not to worry.

69
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,230
 You can even come up on deck for a breath of fresh air.

70
00:08:53,230 --> 00:08:55,830
 But look out that the admiral doesn't see you.

71
00:08:55,990 --> 00:08:57,570
 He's in a foul mood these days.

72
00:08:59,890 --> 00:09:02,770
 Still no lands come in sight.

73
00:09:03,030 --> 00:09:05,010
 The men are becoming restless, difficult to handle.

74
00:09:10,670 --> 00:09:15,250
 London's sign, my friends.

75
00:09:15,330 --> 00:09:16,480
 It means lands very near.

76
00:09:31,850 --> 00:09:48,450
 Think to your calculations, Admiral.

77
00:09:50,080 --> 00:09:52,180
 In my opinion, we had better change course.

78
00:09:52,280 --> 00:09:53,700
 Sail more to the west-southwest.

79
00:09:53,860 --> 00:09:54,760
 Land should be there.

80
00:09:54,990 --> 00:09:55,620
 Why do that?

81
00:09:55,700 --> 00:09:57,120
 Well, observe the flight of the birds.

82
00:09:58,880 --> 00:10:00,570
 We must let them guide us out.

83
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:04,540
 So, we're agreeing.

84
00:10:04,680 --> 00:10:07,300
 Columbus failed to discover America and the Florida coast,

85
00:10:07,380 --> 00:10:08,940
 but only a small island in the Bahamas.

86
00:10:09,850 --> 00:10:11,270
 Why did Columbus do that?

87
00:10:11,270 --> 00:10:12,350
 Why? Tell us, Maestro.

88
00:10:12,670 --> 00:10:14,450
 No, you have to see it from his point of view.

89
00:10:14,550 --> 00:10:16,330
 For two months, they had been sailing.

90
00:10:16,450 --> 00:10:17,990
 By now, they didn't know where they were.

91
00:10:18,090 --> 00:10:19,540
 No landmarks, no sign.

92
00:10:19,820 --> 00:10:21,260
 Any welcome change was...

93
00:10:21,260 --> 00:10:23,860
 And I'd say he's got serious problems with the crew.

94
00:10:23,980 --> 00:10:25,220
 Oh, yes, to say the least.

95
00:10:26,010 --> 00:10:28,790
 With this poor excuse for an admiral.

96
00:10:28,790 --> 00:10:30,830
 Yeah, you're right. He doesn't even know where he's going.

97
00:10:30,930 --> 00:10:32,690
 He's got us into a real mess, he has.

98
00:10:33,040 --> 00:10:35,070
 Not one among us will make it home again.

99
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:38,910
 Come on, string him up.

100
00:10:39,010 --> 00:10:41,490
 No, a pair of lead boots and lower it into the water.

101
00:10:42,300 --> 00:10:43,460
 But then what?

102
00:10:43,520 --> 00:10:46,220
 All that bunch on the Neen on the Pinta, they're a rough lot.

103
00:10:46,400 --> 00:10:48,140
 Yeah, the ones on the pinta especially.

104
00:10:55,180 --> 00:11:00,630
 Friends, I thank you all.

105
00:11:00,830 --> 00:11:02,470
 You have all shown great patience.

106
00:11:02,750 --> 00:11:04,290
 We can't be far from land now.

107
00:11:04,490 --> 00:11:10,450
 It's a matter of hours, and the first to sight land will receive 10,000 maravides from our gracious queen.

108
00:11:42,590 --> 00:12:06,550
 Near Colombia, you will not fight Juan Rodriguez.

109
00:12:06,800 --> 00:12:07,580
 What reward?

110
00:12:08,160 --> 00:12:11,470
 The first man to sight land would receive 10,000.

111
00:12:11,470 --> 00:12:14,760
 That was the first to slow coming from land.

112
00:12:14,880 --> 00:12:32,490
 Ask these gentle islands around Cipango.

113
00:12:48,860 --> 00:13:30,080
 Possession of the pre-named Holy Saviour San Salvador.

114
00:14:03,450 --> 00:14:20,400
 Cipango is the city with roofs of gold.

115
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:23,200
 "Coste usted, Cipango, la ciudad de oro."

116
00:14:23,200 --> 00:14:27,420
 "Oye, what are you?"

117
00:14:27,420 --> 00:14:30,420
 "Baca, parato, bula, pagoteca."

118
00:14:30,420 --> 00:14:32,670
 "The man says if we go south, we locate."

119
00:14:32,670 --> 00:14:35,170
 "And the gold? Where do we find it?"

120
00:14:35,170 --> 00:14:39,170
 "Parato, amongo, atopolo, baragogo."

121
00:14:39,170 --> 00:14:40,230
 "We go south."

122
00:14:40,230 --> 00:14:43,230
 "This man says in the south a powerful king possesses vases of gold."

123
00:14:43,230 --> 00:14:48,050
 "Look here, my man. Ask all of them."

124
00:14:48,050 --> 00:14:51,360
 "Hoyol, hodemo, yohol, homogo, homongo."

125
00:14:51,360 --> 00:14:56,060
 After exploring the Bahamas, Columbus sailed for what he thought was Cipango.

126
00:14:56,060 --> 00:14:59,430
 It was really Cuba.

127
00:14:59,430 --> 00:15:03,990
 There he is to encounter a strange thing, natives smoking a sort of dried leaf.

128
00:15:05,850 --> 00:15:10,520
 And that was to be one of the discoveries that he brought back from America.

129
00:15:11,060 --> 00:15:14,100
 Hardly one of the best ones, I assure you, but then there we are.

130
00:15:20,970 --> 00:15:25,170
 Land, Hispaniola.

131
00:15:25,410 --> 00:15:27,720
 In fact, it was Haiti or Santo Bingo.

132
00:15:37,190 --> 00:15:42,940
 All these Indians don't look very Asian.

133
00:15:50,540 --> 00:15:57,020
 To see, 1492, while the crew was having a fine time, the Santa Maria somehow ran aground on a coral reef.

134
00:15:57,440 --> 00:15:59,440
 And so they'll have to abandon the ship there.

135
00:15:59,440 --> 00:16:05,870
 And since there isn't enough room for everybody in the two remaining ships, well, they found a colony, Villa de Navidad.

136
00:16:06,330 --> 00:16:07,610
 You will have an easy life.

137
00:16:08,230 --> 00:16:09,030
 The people are gentle.

138
00:16:09,350 --> 00:16:10,340
 Don't be brutal with them.

139
00:16:10,820 --> 00:16:13,440
 Collect some gold, well, let's say a barrel, and spices.

140
00:16:13,840 --> 00:16:16,520
 When we return with reinforcements, we will resume conquest.

141
00:16:16,660 --> 00:16:17,900
 Admiral, don't you worry.

142
00:16:18,100 --> 00:16:19,560
 I'll take personal charge.

143
00:16:22,450 --> 00:16:31,960
 14th of March, 1493, 224 days after his departure, Christopher Columbus returned to Palos, and he had discovered an immense continent.

144
00:16:31,960 --> 00:16:37,930
 He brought back no gold, but rather, he brought back maize, corn, maniak, tobacco, and Indians.

145
00:16:38,270 --> 00:17:49,880
 Ah, those Indians have asked me to transmit to you of your science you have shown,

146
00:17:50,110 --> 00:17:52,320
 which appears beyond the reach of mere mortal.

147
00:17:56,700 --> 00:18:00,330
 There are no man, Spain, who could have accomplished this.

148
00:18:00,450 --> 00:18:04,350
 Gentlemen, would any of you be able to make this egg that I hold stand up on end?

149
00:18:20,300 --> 00:18:26,690
 One can do anything once it's performed by another.

150
00:18:26,960 --> 00:18:28,250
 And what's your reply to that?

151
00:18:28,250 --> 00:18:35,410
 Months later, in September, 1493, a full fleet leaves Cadiz commanded by Columbus,

152
00:18:35,590 --> 00:18:40,570
 17 caravels and 1,200 men, including farmers, representatives of the various trades,

153
00:18:40,850 --> 00:18:45,610
 20 horsemen, sheep, seeds and tools, two brothers and six monks, including Bernard Boyle.

154
00:18:45,900 --> 00:18:49,070
 Oh, yes, and there he is, our friend, Piero. He's on the ship's crew this time.

155
00:18:49,370 --> 00:18:50,730
 Well, he's the cabin boy.

156
00:19:29,600 --> 00:19:33,510
 Spaniola, city of Navidad.

157
00:19:37,750 --> 00:19:43,940
 The supervisor and his men were a little too greedy and too grabby.

158
00:19:44,780 --> 00:19:48,460
 Only the gentle natives had had enough of it.

159
00:19:56,150 --> 00:20:01,460
 Right here, we will name it Isabella.

160
00:20:01,460 --> 00:20:04,960
 Since no more natives will help us, we will work with our bare hands.

161
00:20:05,200 --> 00:20:08,570
 We work with our bare hands. I would rather go to Spain, Admiral.

162
00:20:08,850 --> 00:20:10,290
 I shall go on board at once.

163
00:20:11,220 --> 00:20:13,280
 Dear friends, these lands belong to you.

164
00:20:13,400 --> 00:20:16,820
 As for me, I must return to inform their majesties what has happened.

165
00:20:17,020 --> 00:20:18,520
 But I shall return as soon as I can.

166
00:20:18,720 --> 00:20:22,650
 While I'm gone, brothers Diego and Bartolomeo will look after you all.

167
00:20:30,700 --> 00:20:37,610
 Of penance, Columbus is at last able to set off on his third voyage.

168
00:20:38,190 --> 00:20:43,430
 He takes with him 300 volunteers, 30 of them women, to provide good stock.

169
00:20:43,430 --> 00:20:47,590
 And this time, he skirts Venezuela and goes on to Hispaniola.

170
00:20:48,700 --> 00:20:50,990
 I can hardly wait to see Isabella.

171
00:20:51,230 --> 00:20:54,770
 After all this time, surely it has become a thriving community, a real colony.

172
00:21:01,570 --> 00:21:06,300
 Diego.

173
00:21:06,890 --> 00:21:07,710
 What joy.

174
00:21:08,450 --> 00:21:08,940
 Brother.

175
00:21:10,630 --> 00:21:11,070
 Tolomeo.

176
00:21:11,380 --> 00:21:12,640
 In the name of God, what's happened?

177
00:21:12,790 --> 00:21:13,920
 They refuse to obey us.

178
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:15,360
 Obey anyone from Genoa, never.

179
00:21:15,540 --> 00:21:17,320
 We haven't any authority over them at all.

180
00:21:17,460 --> 00:21:18,180
 We can do nothing.

181
00:21:18,340 --> 00:21:20,780
 And they treat the Indians cruelly, steal their jewels.

182
00:21:21,080 --> 00:21:22,240
 And run after their women.

183
00:21:22,580 --> 00:21:23,620
 It's been sickness.

184
00:21:24,170 --> 00:21:26,460
 But what can I report to their majesties?

185
00:21:26,660 --> 00:21:28,350
 I'm in a very difficult position now.

186
00:21:28,350 --> 00:21:31,470
 Easy, but you have ships.

187
00:21:31,650 --> 00:21:32,890
 Why not take them as slaves?

188
00:21:39,210 --> 00:21:50,170
 The admiral who tries to play explorer.

189
00:21:50,450 --> 00:21:53,810
 Majesty, just what are we to do with these poor people?

190
00:21:53,930 --> 00:21:54,730
 What are your orders?

191
00:21:54,950 --> 00:21:56,890
 You will liberate them all, miserable souls.

192
00:21:57,110 --> 00:21:59,470
 Return them to their home, Hispaniola, at once.

193
00:21:59,930 --> 00:22:01,650
 Francisco Bodabia to the palace.

194
00:22:03,300 --> 00:22:06,930
 Appoint you governor of Hispaniola to replace Viceroy Columbus.

195
00:22:07,290 --> 00:22:10,850
 You will discover what the situation is there and dispatch a report to me directly.

196
00:22:11,170 --> 00:22:11,790
 Do you understand?

197
00:22:12,030 --> 00:22:12,790
 Now off you go.

198
00:22:12,790 --> 00:22:20,700
 The 24th of August, 1500, Bodabia arrived in Hispaniola.

199
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:24,580
 His first act was to send Diego and Bartolomeo to prison.

200
00:22:27,180 --> 00:22:29,800
 And Columbus, where's Christopher Columbus?

201
00:22:30,720 --> 00:22:32,700
 Governor, we have arrested him.

202
00:22:33,020 --> 00:22:34,010
 Is he in irons?

203
00:22:34,210 --> 00:22:36,410
 Nobody here would dare do such a thing to him, Governor.

204
00:22:37,030 --> 00:22:39,540
 I'll do it. I swear I'll enjoy it.

205
00:22:40,810 --> 00:22:51,260
 Let me take your chains off. They're painful.

206
00:22:51,380 --> 00:22:52,440
 No, I'd rather wear them.

207
00:22:52,500 --> 00:22:52,920
 You would?

208
00:22:54,040 --> 00:22:56,350
 Truly, thank you. This way is better.

209
00:22:56,350 --> 00:22:59,790
 Poor Columbus. As a result, he disembarked a caddy's wearing chains.

210
00:23:00,340 --> 00:23:02,580
 Unfortunately, he was released on the orders of the Queen.

211
00:23:03,500 --> 00:23:07,320
 As Isabella received him with apologies, Budabia had overstepped his orders,

212
00:23:07,520 --> 00:23:11,620
 but there was no way for our friend to regain his title of viceroy, a governor.

213
00:23:12,080 --> 00:23:17,470
 He returned and retired to a Franciscan monastery and began a period of penance.

214
00:23:18,730 --> 00:23:21,150
 Well, I suppose that was about all the expeditions.

215
00:23:21,310 --> 00:23:24,670
 No, in 152, he was to leave again on his fourth, his last voyage.

216
00:23:24,670 --> 00:23:27,210
 It would prove to be the most painful of all.

217
00:23:28,030 --> 00:23:36,780
 Hurricanes and sickness, betrayals, famine, mutinies.

218
00:23:39,720 --> 00:23:44,390
 Yes, yes, he was spared no ordeal.

219
00:23:45,640 --> 00:23:49,260
 There isn't a hair on my body that hasn't turned white.

220
00:23:49,600 --> 00:23:51,340
 My body's exhausted, I can't go on.

221
00:23:51,620 --> 00:23:53,440
 How I long to see the dear land of Spain again.

222
00:23:53,820 --> 00:23:54,780
 How much I need sleep.

223
00:23:54,940 --> 00:23:59,560
 Two years after his return, Queen Isabella died two more years, and Columbus died too.

224
00:23:59,900 --> 00:24:00,960
 He was almost blind.

225
00:24:01,300 --> 00:24:04,440
 He paralyzed, miserable, and abandoned by everybody.

226
00:24:04,860 --> 00:24:05,840
 Well, nearly everybody.

227
00:24:09,150 --> 00:24:12,450
 See more than those islands, my children.

228
00:24:12,770 --> 00:24:15,030
 Cipango was in our grass, we nearly discovered it.

229
00:24:15,370 --> 00:24:17,330
 Yes, if we'd only...

230
00:24:17,330 --> 00:24:21,950
 Hardly a very pleasant ending for an explorer, wouldn't you say?

231
00:24:21,950 --> 00:24:28,380
 But do you realize it was the willpower and the perseverance of Columbus that truly opened the American continent to the world?

